From 81ac96c7a038ac5768642b5236b320c2742658a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:15:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/23] BananaDS: the JIT's code memory on 2 MB huge pages
 (melonDS patch 0021, jit.hugepages)

melonDS's AArch64 JIT writes its code into a 32 MB slice of a static array aligned to 16 KB, and its reset
fills all of it, so all of it is resident anyway. Patch 0021 aligns the array to 2 MB and madvise()s it
MADV_HUGEPAGE before the first touch: BananaLinux's kernel (THP in madvise mode, 4 KB pages) then maps the
JIT's stubs, blocks and far region with 2 MB pages, a few TLB entries instead of one per 4 KB page. The JIT's
first reset logs how much of the slice the kernel gave huge pages. jit.hugepages=off (MELONDS_JIT_HUGEPAGE=0)
is the old layout; emulation doesn't change either way.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PvbFhKAw3ERk5a13S651g1
